+*   **Subdivisions:** Not required for dense datasets; used for sparse datasets to improve interpolation [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Poor Interpolation with Standard MLPs:** Using the same neural network architecture as NeRF (a 5D radiance field MLP) to parameterize a light field (4D ray-space) leads to poor interpolation quality for view synthesis. This is because learning a function on 4D ray space from partial 2D slices is significantly more challenging than learning 3D density [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Convex Hull Assumption:** The method assumes radiance is constant along rays. This holds when the convex hull of the scene geometry does not contain any viewpoints used for rendering. If this assumption is violated, the single-evaluation-per-pixel model may produce artifacts [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Sparse Input Interpolation:** The embedding network alone does not interpolate well in sparser input sequences. Local light fields are required to mitigate large depth and texture changes [S1, S1, unofficial].
