 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Speed:** Supports rendering with a single network evaluation per pixel for small baseline light fields, and only a few evaluations per pixel for larger baselines. This is significantly faster than NeRF, which requires hundreds of evaluations per ray [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Maintains a small memory footprint compared to volumetric representations [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Quality:** Achieves state-of-the-art view synthesis quality on dense forward-facing datasets (e.g., Stanford Light Fields) [S1, S1, unofficial]. For sparse inputs, it achieves results competitive with NeRF-based approaches [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast, memory efficient, excels at modeling complicated view-dependent effects (e.g., distorted reflections and refractions) [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that radiance is constant along rays. Performance on sparse inputs requires the complexity of local light fields [S1, S1, unofficial].
-*   **Comparison to AutoInt:** Unlike AutoInt, which predicts integrated radiance along ray segments and uses subdivision but has lower accuracy than NeRF due to approximation of the nested volume rendering integral, this approach leads to comparable performance to NeRF [S1, S1, unofficial].
